{"protocol":"mindblown-brain/1","brain":"programming","page":{"id":"lang/rust/ownership","title":"Ownership and borrowing","type":"concept","lobe":"lang","desc":"Each value has exactly one owner; references borrow it either shared (many readers) or mutable (one writer), never both at once, and the borrow checker proves lifetimes line up.","intro":"Each value has exactly one owner; references borrow it either shared (many readers) or mutable (one writer), never both at once, and the borrow checker proves lifetimes line up.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":[{"id":"lang/rust/ownership#1","page":"lang/rust/ownership","lead":"One owner, moved not copied","text":"Assigning a non-Copy value moves it; using the old binding afterwards is a compile error.","section":"Synapses","links":["lang/c/pointers"],"cites":["https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/","pplx://sessions/d6c69c92-bde0-762d-7472-b4955224f003"]},{"id":"lang/rust/ownership#2","page":"lang/rust/ownership","lead":"Shared XOR mutable","text":"At any point you may have any number of &T or exactly one &mut T; this rule is what rules out data races and iterator invalidation.","section":"Synapses","links":[],"cites":["https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/"]},{"id":"lang/rust/ownership#3","page":"lang/rust/ownership","lead":"Lifetimes are a type-level fact","text":"Lifetime annotations tell the compiler how long references must be valid; most are inferred by elision rules.","section":"Synapses","links":["para/type-systems/inference"],"cites":["https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/"]}],"sources":["https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/","pplx://sessions/d6c69c92-bde0-762d-7472-b4955224f003"],"sections":["Synapses"],"parent":"lang/rust"},"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=lang%2Frust%2Fownership","parent":{"id":"lang/rust","title":"Rust","type":"language","lobe":"lang","parent":null,"desc":"A systems language started by Graydon Hoare at Mozilla, 1.0 in 2015; memory safety without a garbage collector throug